// The sdf package: signed distance fields, shapes as functions from a point
// to its distance from the surface (negative inside). A program reaches it
// with 'import sdf', and gets:
//
// - Primitives (primitive.cone): 'sphere', 'box', 'roundBox', 'capsule',
//   'torus', 'cylinder', 'cone', 'roundCone', 'plane', 'ellipsoid'.
// - Operators on distances (operator.cone): 'union', 'subtract',
//   'intersect', their smooth forms 'smoothUnion', 'smoothSubtract',
//   'smoothIntersect' (Quilez's quadratic smooth minimum) and
//   'smoothUnionBlend' (with the material blend factor); 'onion', 'round',
//   'exclusive'.
// - Operators on the point (domain.cone): 'translate', 'rotate',
//   'scalePoint' with 'scaleDistance', 'Placement' (all three);
//   'mirrorX', 'mirrorY', 'mirrorZ', 'mirror'; 'repeat', 'repeatLimited';
//   'elongate'.
// - hg_sdf's fillets (fillet.cone, MIT): 'unionRound', 'intersectRound',
//   'subtractRound', 'unionChamfer', 'unionColumns', 'unionStairs',
//   'intersectStairs', 'subtractStairs', 'pipe', 'groove', 'tongue',
//   'engrave'.
// - Repetition along a curve (curve.cone): the Curve trait, 'Arc' (a
//   circular arc, with 'project' and 'cells') and 'PathCurve' (a
//   sculpt.Path), the Repeated trait, and 'repeatAlong' and
//   'repeatAlongArc', a shape repeated in the curve's rotation-minimizing
//   frames, the exact union of every copy.
// - The horn (horn.cone): 'Horn', a ribbed, tapering, curling horn, the
//   union of its cells along an Arc.
// - The Shape trait (shape.cone), 'FnShape' (a function as a Shape), and
//   'gradient' and 'normal' by finite differences.
// - Detail from noise (detail.cone): 'displace' (not a distance) and
//   'fbmDetail' (Quilez's fBm of spheres, a bound).
//
// Numbers are f32, points geomath's Vec3. A shape is written as code:
//
//   fn scene(p Vec3) f32 {
//     imm body = sphere(p, 1.);
//     imm hole = cylinder(rotate(p, turn), 2., 0.4);
//     smoothSubtract(body, hole, 0.1);
//   }
//
// EXACT AND BOUNDED. A distance is exact when it is the Euclidean distance
// to the surface, a bound when it never exceeds it (a sphere tracer stays
// safe, and a mesher finds the same surface). Each function's comment says
// which it gives; the tests sample the gradient's length (at most 1 for
// either) for those that claim one.
//
// THE REPRESENTATION IS NOT DECIDED. Shapes are composed as functions here,
// on the CPU and, the same functions in the same order, in sdf.slang. Every
// function is one node of an operator graph (a primitive with its sizes, an
// operator with its constants, a domain operator on the point), so a data
// tree that can be sent and evaluated can be built over these later, if
// that is what crosses the network.
//
// sdf.slang holds the same functions for shaders, under the same names
// (all but PathCurve; the constructors are functions there, 'makeArc',
// 'makeArcCells', 'makeHorn'; the traits are interfaces and the generic
// functions Slang generics over them). The two are kept in step by hand;
// tests/parity.cone checks them against each other on a GPU. Those without
// square roots, division or trigonometry agree bit for bit, the rest to
// within 1.5e-5; the README has what was measured.
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports:
//
//   scalar.cone     helpers, Blend, CurveFrame
//   primitive.cone  the primitives
//   operator.cone   union, subtraction, intersection, smooth and hard; onion, round
//   domain.cone     translation, rotation, scale, mirrors, repetition, elongation
//   fillet.cone     hg_sdf's fillets (MIT)
//   curve.cone      Curve, Arc, PathCurve, Repeated, repeatAlong
//   horn.cone       Horn
//   shape.cone      Shape, FnShape, gradient, normal
//   detail.cone     displace, latticeSpheres, fbmDetail

mod sdf;

import libc;
import geomath use *;
import collections use *;
import sculpt;
import noise;
